Caisson Drive
Ingredients: PROGRESSIVE HOUSE + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + Double Bass
Creative Catalyst: The genre was born at an illegal rooftop rave on a half-submerged brutalist parking structure in a flooded megacity, 2091.

Caisson Drive is an architectural, slow-burn electronic subgenre that welds the marathon hypnotic propulsion of classic progressive house to the punishing, unvarnished weight of brutalist industrialism. Its beating heart is not an electronic sub-oscillator, but an acoustic double bass miked raw and forced through overdriven preamp circuits—slapped, bowed sul ponticello, and physically rattled until wood and gut resonate like structural concrete under hydraulic stress.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 122–126 BPM. A locked, relentless four-on-the-floor groove propelled by a mechanical swing; the acoustic double bass provides micro-rhythmic ghost notes, fingerboard clatter, and visceral percussive slap that sit just ahead of the kick.
- Harmonic Palette: Dorian and Phrygian modes, rooted in stark open-string double bass fundamentals (C, D, and G). Bleak minor ninths and suspended fourths drift across slowly cycling, unpolished analog synth chords, spiced with microtonal friction from bowed acoustic harmonics.
- Production Signatures: (1) Dual-transduced double bass routing—combining a dry contact pickup pushed into germanium transistor fuzz with a room microphone capturing natural spruce-top slap, (2) Convolution reverbs modeled exclusively on flooded subterranean concrete tanks, stairwells, and hollow elevator shafts, (3) Anti-drop arrangement ethos: no sweeping white noise or sudden snare rolls, relying instead on gradual acoustic bow pressure, tape-loop degradation, and eight-minute linear filtration swells.
- Dynamics: Monolithic and patient. Tracks breathe like surging coastal tides, stacking low-mid acoustic heft and sandpaper-dry percussion into towering, claustrophobic structures that release pressure through resonant bow glissandos rather than electronic drops.

Scene & Origin Lore
In the late autumn of 2091, as sea walls yielded along the Lower Batavia Estuary, stranded youth and displaced engineers began converting the dry upper tiers of the half-drowned Telecom Parking Structure into illicit sonic sanctuaries. Hauling salvaged upright basses, diesel generators, and tube stacks up forty flights of spiral ramps, they established Caisson Drive as an act of physical defiance against structural decay. Fashion in the scene is defined by utilitarian survival gear: salt-stained neoprene dive smocks, industrial safety carabiners, heavy wool fisherman collars, and boots lined with recycled insulation. Ravers gather in silence until the first 909 kick strikes, at which point the ritual demands placing both palms flat against the cold concrete balustrades, feeling the seismic sub-harmonics of the double bass vibrate straight down through the flooded building pilings into the dark water below.

Typical Instruments: 3/4 German Acoustic Double Bass (wound with heavy gut strings and equipped with custom copper contact pickups), Roland TR-909 (routed through overdriven tube preamps), Korg MS-20 (for harsh upper-mid bandpass noise), bowed waterphone, reel-to-reel magnetic tape delays, and hydrophone field recordings of tidal surges.

For Fans Of: Sasha & John Digweed (the darkest Northern Exposure sessions), Colin Stetson, Kas:st, Sandwell District.

